      "Not quite — the phrase you’re thinking of is **“Catch-22,” not “Catch-23.”**\n\n---\n\n## 📘 What is “Catch-22”?\n\n**entity[\"book\",\"Catch-22\",\"novel by Joseph Heller\"]** is a famous novel, and the term has become part of everyday language.\n\n### 🔁 Meaning:\nA **Catch-22** is a situation where:\n- You’re stuck in a **no-win loop**\n- The **rules contradict each other**\n- Solving the problem is impossible because the **solution depends on the problem itself**\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Simple Example\n\n> You need experience to get a job  \n> But you need a job to get experience  \n\n➡️ That’s a **Catch-22**\n\n---\n\n## ✈️ Origin (from the novel)\n\nimage_group{\"aspect_ratio\":\"1:1\",\"query\":[\"Catch-22 novel cover Joseph Heller\",\"WW2 bomber crew novel scene Catch-22\",\"Joseph Heller portrait\",\"Catch-22 satire war absurdity illustration\"]}\n\nIn the story:\n- A pilot can avoid dangerous missions if he is **mentally unfit**\n- But if he *asks* to be excused, it proves he is **sane enough to fly**\n\n➡️ So he can **never escape**  \n➡️ That’s the Catch-22\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ So what about “Catch-23”?\n\n- ❌ Not a standard phrase  \n- ❌ Not used in English idioms  \n- ❌ Likely a **misheard or mistaken version**\n\n---\n\n## 💡 In one line\n\n> **Catch-22 = a trap where every option blocks the other**\n\n---\n\nIf you want, I can map this idea to real-world systems (business, AI governance, or your AUMNITY workflows) — there are actually many hidden “Catch-22 loops” there."
     ]
