      "You’re close — there are a few **“catch / code / number” phrases** people mix up when talking about crisis situations. Let’s separate them cleanly:\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 1. **Catch-22** (most likely what you mean)\n**entity[\"book\",\"Catch-22\",\"novel by Joseph Heller\"]**\n\n- Not exactly “handling a crisis”\n- Means being **trapped in a no-win situation**\n- Often *feels* like a crisis because there’s no clear way out\n\n👉 Example:  \nYou can’t fix the problem because the system itself blocks the fix\n\n---\n\n## 🚨 2. **Code Red** (actual crisis phrase)\n**entity[\"emergency_code\",\"Code Red\",\"emergency alert level\"]**\n\n- Used in **hospitals, military, organizations**\n- Means **immediate danger / emergency**\n- This is closest to what you’re describing\n\n👉 Example:  \n“System failure — Code Red, all teams respond now”\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ 3. **Catch-23**\n- ❌ Not a real or standard phrase  \n- Usually a **misremembered version of Catch-22**\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 4. Other related phrases (people sometimes confuse)\n\n- **“Crisis mode”** → general term, no number  \n- **“Red alert”** → high danger situation  \n- **“Zero hour”** → critical moment  \n- **“11th hour”** → last-minute crisis\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Quick clarity\n\n| Phrase       | Meaning                          | Use case                  |\n|-------------|----------------------------------|---------------------------|\n| Catch-22    | No-win logical trap              | Decision/system problem   |\n| Code Red    | Immediate crisis/emergency       | Real-world response       |\n| Catch-23    | ❌ Not real                      | Misheard                  |\n\n---\n\n### 🧭 Bottom line\nIf you mean **“handling a crisis urgently” → say *Code Red***  \nIf you mean **“stuck with no solution” → say *Catch-22***\n\n---\n\nIf you want, describe the exact situation you have in mind — I’ll tell you the *precise phrase professionals use* (business / military / AI systems context)."
     ]
